      "text": "To find the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\), we start by recognizing that \\(\\angle ADB = 180^\\circ\\) because \\(D\\) is a point on the circle and \\(AB\\) is a chord, making \\(\\angle ADB\\) an inscribed angle that intercepts the same arc as the central angle \\(\\angle ACB\\).\n\nGiven that \\(\\angle ADB = 180^\\circ\\), and knowing that the sum of the angles in triangle \\(ABD\\) is \\(180^\\circ\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\angle ABD + \\angle ADB + \\angle BAD = 180^\\circ\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n130^\\circ + 180^\\circ + \\angle BAD = 180^\\circ\n\\]\nSolving for \\(\\angle BAD\\):\n\\[\n\\angle BAD = 180^\\circ - 130^\\circ - 180^\\circ = -110^\\circ + 180^\\circ = 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\angle ADB = 180^\\circ\\), and \\(\\angle ABD = 93^\\circ\\), the remaining angle \\(\\angle ABD\\) and \\(\\angle DAB\\) in triangle \\(ABD\\) must add up to \\(180^\\circ\\). We already know \\(\\angle ABD = 93^\\circ\\), so:\n\\[\n\\angle DAB = 180^\\circ - 93^\\circ - 50^\\circ = 37^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\angle ACB\\). We know that \\(\\angle ABD\\) and \\(\\angle DAB\\) are adjacent angles on a straight line, and together they form \\(\\angle BAC\\). Thus:\n\\[\n\\angle BAC = \\angle ADB - \\angle DAB = 180^\\circ - 93^\\circ - 37^\\circ = 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\nFinally, since \\(\\angle BAC = \\angle ACB\\), the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{50}\n\\]",
