      "text": "To solve for the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) in the given diagram, we start by identifying and analyzing the given angles and their relationships.\n\n1. **Identify the given angles:**\n   - \\(\\angle ABD = 93^\\circ\\)\n   - \\(\\angle DBC = 130^\\circ\\)\n\n2. **Understand the triangle properties:**\n   - We have a triangle \\(ABD\\) and a triangle \\(ABC\\).\n   - The sum of the angles in a triangle is always \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\n3. **Determine the measure of \\(\\angle ABD\\):**\n   - Given \\(\\angle ABD = 93^\\circ\\).\n\n4. **Consider the triangle \\(ABD\\):**\n   - Let \\(\\angle BAD = x\\).\n   - Since the sum of the angles in triangle \\(ABD\\) is \\(180^\\circ\\), we have:\n     \\[\n     \\angle BAD + \\angle ABD + \\angle ADB = 180^\\circ\n     \\]\n     Substituting the known values:\n     \\[\n     x + 93^\\circ + \\angle ADB = 180^\\circ\n     \\]\n     Solving for \\(\\angle ADB\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle ADB = 180^\\circ - x - 93^\\circ = 87^\\circ - x\n     \\]\n\n5. **Consider the triangle \\(ABC\\):**\n   - Let \\(\\angle BAC = y\\).\n   - Since \\(\\angle DBC = 130^\\circ\\), and knowing that \\(\\angle DBC\\) is an exterior angle to triangle \\(ABD\\), it must equal the sum of the two non-adjacent interior angles of triangle \\(ABD\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle DBC = \\angle BAC + \\angle ABD\n     \\]\n     Substituting the known values:\n     \\[\n     130^\\circ = y + 93^\\circ\n     \\]\n     Solving for \\(y\\):\n     \\[\n     y = 130^\\circ - 93^\\circ = 37^\\circ\n     \\]\n\n6. **Determine the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\):**\n   - Now, we need to find \\(\\angle ACB\\). In triangle \\(ABC\\), the sum of the angles is \\(180^\\circ\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle BAC + \\angle ABC + \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ\n     \\]\n     We already know \\(\\angle BAC = 37^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle ABD = 93^\\circ\\). The angle \\(\\angle ABC\\) can be found using the fact that \\(\\angle ABD\\) and \\(\\angle ABC\\) are supplementary (they form a linear pair):\n     \\[\n     \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - \\angle ABD = 180^\\circ - 93^\\circ = 87^\\circ\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) is:\n     \\[\n     \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC - \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - 37^\\circ - 87^\\circ = 56^\\circ\n     \\]\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
