      "text": "[asy]\nsize(250);\ndraw((-60,0)--(0,0));\ndraw((0,0)--(64.3,76.6)--(166,0)--cycle);\nlabel(\"$A$\",(64.3,76.6),N);\nlabel(\"$93^\\circ$\",(64.3,73),S);\nlabel(\"$130^\\circ$\",(0,0),NW);\nlabel(\"$B$\",(0,0),S);\nlabel(\"$D$\",(-60,0),S);\nlabel(\"$C$\",(166,0),S);\n[/asy]\n\nTo find the measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\), we will use the properties of angles in a triangle and the given angle measures. Here is the step-by-step reasoning and calculation:\n\n1. **Identify the given angles and their relationships:**\n   - We know \\(\\angle ABD = 93^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle BAC = 130^\\circ\\).\n   - The angle \\(\\angle ADB = 180^\\circ\\) because they are supplementary angles (they form a straight line).\n\n2. **Calculate \\(\\angle ADC\\):**\n   - Since \\(\\angle ADB = 180^\\circ\\), we can find \\(\\angle ADC\\) (which is the exterior angle at point \\(D\\)):\n     \\[\n     \\angle ADC = 180^\\circ - \\angle ADB = 180^\\circ - 180^\\circ = 130^\\circ\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, \\(\\angle ADC = 130^\\circ\\).\n\n3. **Determine the measure of \\(\\angle BAC\\):**\n   - We already know \\(\\angle BAC = 130^\\circ\\).\n\n4. **Calculate \\(\\angle ACB\\):**\n   - We need to find \\(\\angle ACB\\), which is one of the angles in \\(\\triangle ABC\\).\n   - The sum of the angles in any triangle is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n   - Let \\(\\angle ACB = x\\). Then:\n     \\[\n     \\angle BAC + \\angle ABC + \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ\n     \\]\n   - We know \\(\\angle BAC = 130^\\circ\\) and \\(\\angle ADC = 130^\\circ\\), but we need to find \\(\\angle ABC\\).\n   - Since \\(\\angle ABC\\) is an interior angle of \\(\\triangle ABC\\) and we do not have any direct information about it, we will use the exterior angle property again:\n     \\[\n     \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - \\angle BAC - \\angle ACB\n     \\]\n   - However, we can use the fact that \\(\\angle ABC\\) is the remaining angle in the triangle and we can find it by subtracting the known angles from \\(180^\\circ\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - 130^\\circ - x\n     \\]\n   - But we need to find \\(\\angle ACB\\) directly. Since \\(\\angle ACB\\) is an external angle to the triangle at point \\(C\\):\n     \\[\n     \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - \\angle ABC - \\angle BAC\n     \\]\n   - We need to find \\(\\angle ABC\\) first:\n     \\[\n     \\angle ABC = 180^\\circ - 130^\\circ - x\n     \\]\n   - But we can use the fact that \\(\\angle ACB\\) is the remaining angle in the triangle:\n     \\[\n     \\angle ACB = 180^\\circ - 130^\\circ - 50^\\circ\n     \\]\n     (where \\(50^\\circ\\) is the remaining angle when we subtract the known angles from \\(180^\\circ\\)).\n\n5. **Final calculation:**\n   - Therefore, \\(\\angle ACB = 80^\\circ\\).\n\nThe measure of \\(\\angle ACB\\) is \\(\\boxed{80^\\circ}\\).",
